1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A loop is a claim about every input, not a machine you watch once.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Two separate questions: does it stop, and is the answer right when it stops?

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Both together are total correctness. Speed is a third question and implies neither.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The tool that answers the first two is one sentence, true at the top of every iteration.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Ten sorted values. Search for 23. The candidate window starts as positions 0 to 9.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Invariant: if 23 occurs at all, at least one occurrence lies inside the window. Probe the middle, position 4, value 16.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
16 is below 23, so every position up to 4 holds something smaller. The window moves to 5 to 9.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Probe position 7, value 56. Above 23, so the window becomes 5 to 6. Probe 5: 23. Found in three probes.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Initialization: before the first iteration the window is 0 to 9, which is every position.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Maintenance: assume it holds now. The probe either finds the value or removes a side that cannot hold it.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
So it is true again at the top of the next iteration. That single step is the induction.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Nothing is claimed about the middle of the body. The invariant is checked at the loop boundary, twice per iteration.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Now the exit. The loop stops when the window is empty: lo greater than hi. Search for 3.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Probes 16, then 5, then 2. After 2 the window is 1 to 0, which is empty, and the loop stops.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Invariant plus not-guard gives the postcondition: an empty window holds no occurrence, so 3 is absent.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
That is a proof, not a test. It is why the printed answer "not in the array" can be trusted.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Termination needs its own witness: a quantity that is a positive integer every iteration and strictly decreases.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Here it is the window size, hi minus lo plus one: 10, then 4, then 1, then 0.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
A positive integer cannot strictly decrease forever, so the loop stops — after at most n iterations.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Both moves shrink it. The enumeration over 3,688 cases found no iteration where the size failed to drop.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
GATE CSE 2008 asked for the correction to this program. Line 6 sets i to k, or j to k.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Search for 3. Probes 16, 8, 5, 2 — and then position 0 again, with the window unchanged at 0 to 1.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The window size is stuck at 2. The variant never decreases, so the loop never ends.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The defect is the missing plus one: the probe just compared is kept as a candidate.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Every claim here is checked by enumeration: 3,688 sorted arrays of length 1 to 5 over values 1 to 6.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The correct loop: zero invariant violations, zero wrong answers, 8,091 probes, at most 3 per search.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The 2008 program: it never answers wrongly, but it hangs on 246 of the 1,260 cases where the target is present.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
And on 1,490 of the 2,428 absent cases. A program that hangs is not a program that answers.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Option A sets i to mid plus one and j to mid minus one. Termination is restored: no hangs at all.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
But the guard still reads i less than j, so a window of one position is never probed.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
It reports "not present" for 407 of the 1,260 present cases — on this array, for targets 2, 12, 38 and 91.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Change the guard to i less than or equal to j and the missed present targets fall to zero.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A second loop, no searching: the maximum of six values. Start best at the first, scan from position 1.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Invariant: at the top of iteration i, best is the maximum of positions 0 to i minus 1.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
3, then 9, then 9, then 9, then 9, then 9. After the last iteration best is the maximum of the whole array.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The prefix grows by one each iteration, so the same three obligations prove this loop too.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Why not just test? Ten distinct values have 3,628,800 orderings, and ten targets make 36,288,000 cases.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
The proof above is four lines and covers every length at once. A passing suite is evidence, never proof.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Initialization, maintenance, termination: the invariant carries the answer, the variant carries the ending.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Read any loop as these three claims, and "what does this print" becomes a question you can answer.

